SSH in
    root@ubuntu:~$ ssh bandit4@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220

The password for the next level is stored in the only human-readable file in the inhere directory. Tip: if your terminal is messed up, try the “reset” command.

We first enter the inhere file and see what we are dealing with.
  bandit4@bandit:~$ ls
  inhere
  bandit4@bandit:~$ cd inhere/
  bandit4@bandit:~/inhere$ ls
  -file00  -file01  -file02  -file03  -file04  -file05  -file06  -file07  -file08  -file09

if we try and cat one of the files we get.
  bandit4@bandit:~/inhere$ cat ./-file00
  Y1[�b1���� ��ɂ�D¬1K0
This is unreadable to a human. It would take a while to cat each file and find the human readable password.

We can use the file command to check all of them.
The file command determins the file type.
    bandit4@bandit:~/inhere$ file ./*
    ./-file00: data
    ./-file01: data
    ./-file02: data
    ./-file03: data
    ./-file04: data
    ./-file05: data
    ./-file06: OpenPGP Public Key
    ./-file07: ASCII text
    ./-file08: data
    ./-file09: Motorola S-Record; binary data in text format

As we can see file07 has ASCII text which is human readable. 
    bandit4@bandit:~/inhere$ cat ./-file07
    6C7h9GD8M6ai5nr7wo1RonrzFjj9yIrG

The password is 6C7h9GD8M6ai5nr7wo1RonrzFjj9yIrG
